      title: "Build Claude's Understanding of You",
      paragraphs: [
        "2. Build Claude's Understanding of You\nA capable model can still produce generic work when it lacks context. The more clearly you explain your circumstances, preferences, goals, and working methods, the less time you spend repeating yourself.",
        "The source material describes two complementary ways to establish context:\n1. Bring useful context across from another AI assistant, where supported.\n2. Ask Claude to interview you so that important areas are not overlooked.\n\nThese are starting points, not substitutes for reviewing what is stored.",
        "**2.1 Import useful context from another assistant**\nIf Claude currently offers a memory-import workflow in your account, it may provide instructions or a prompt that you can run in another AI assistant.\n\nA sensible process is:\n1. Open Claude's current settings and look for its memory or personalisation controls.\n2. If an import option is available, read its instructions carefully.\n3. Run the supplied extraction prompt in the previous assistant.\n4. Review the generated context before transferring it.\n5. Remove outdated, inaccurate, sensitive, or irrelevant information.\n6. Import only what you are comfortable retaining in the new environment.\n7. Ask Claude to summarise what it understood so you can correct mistakes.\n\nThe source material notes a potential weakness in automated imports: they can overemphasise work details while missing personal preferences, communication style, and broader goals. An interview can fill those gaps. If an import feature is not currently available, you can still create a concise context document yourself and provide it to Claude.",
        "**2.2 Run a structured personal and work interview**\nInstead of writing a large biography from scratch, ask Claude to interview you one question at a time. This turns context-building into a guided conversation.\n\nThe interview should cover at least these areas:\n\n| Area | What Claude should learn |\n| --- | --- |\n| Personal context | Relevant background, commitments, interests, and constraints |\n| Work | Role, responsibilities, industry, customers, and recurring tasks |\n| Projects | Current priorities, status, deadlines, and important decisions |\n| Goals | Near-term objectives and longer-term direction |\n| Preferences | Tone, length, structure, level of detail, and formatting |\n| Strengths | Areas where you are experienced and can make final judgments |\n| Friction | Tasks you avoid, repeat, find confusing, or spend too long completing |\n| Decision-making | How you compare options, assess risk, and prefer recommendations |\n| Collaboration | How Claude should ask questions, challenge assumptions, and report progress |\n| Boundaries | Information it should not assume, actions it must not take, and topics requiring extra care |",
        "**Copy-and-use prompt: Claude context interview**\n\n```\nI want to build a useful working context so you can assist me consistently\nacross future tasks.\n\nInterview me in stages. Ask one clear question at a time and wait for my\nanswer before moving on. Cover:\n\n1. My personal context and relevant constraints.\n2. My work, role, responsibilities, and industry.\n3. My current projects and priorities.\n4. My short-term and long-term goals.\n5. My skills, strengths, and areas where I need help.\n6. My communication and writing preferences.\n7. How I like to research, plan, make decisions, and execute.\n8. The recurring tasks that consume my time.\n9. My preferred level of detail and how I want you to format outputs.\n10. The assumptions you should avoid and the actions that require my approval.\n\nAsk follow-up questions when an answer is vague or contradictory. Do not\ninvent details. Distinguish facts I have stated from your interpretations.\n\nAt the end, produce:\n- A concise profile of my relevant context.\n- A list of my working preferences.\n- A list of current goals and projects.\n- A list of recurring tasks you could help with.\n- A list of uncertainties I should clarify.\n\nShow me the complete draft and let me correct it before treating it as\nreliable context.\n```",
        "**2.3 Use voice to reduce the effort**\nThe source material recommends speaking answers rather than typing them when a long interview becomes tedious. Voice input can be especially useful for:\n- Explaining your background naturally.\n- Describing a complicated project.\n- Talking through a decision.\n- Listing recurring responsibilities.\n- Explaining how you want a task completed.\n\nYou can use Claude's available voice features or a separate speech-to-text tool. The exact options and quality vary by device and subscription. A useful technique is to speak freely first, then ask Claude to organise the information. Do not force yourself to produce a polished answer while explaining the raw material.",
        "**2.4 Review context instead of blindly trusting it**\nMore context is not automatically better. Old, contradictory, overly detailed, or sensitive context can make assistance worse.\n\nReview the resulting profile and ask:\n- Is this still true?\n- Is this relevant to future work?\n- Is it a fact or an inference?\n- Is any detail unnecessarily sensitive?\n- Does it conflict with something else?\n- Would I be comfortable with this information being used in future responses?\n\nKeep the profile concise enough to remain useful. Update it when your role, goals, or preferences materially change.\n\n**Action:** Complete one context interview. Save the reviewed result somewhere secure and use the account's current personalisation controls to make relevant context available."
      ]
