      title: "Understand the Different Claude Models",
      paragraphs: [
        "3. Understand the Different Claude Models\nDifferent models can have different strengths, response characteristics, and usage costs. Selecting a more capable model for every task can waste usage; selecting a lightweight model for a difficult task can produce weak results or require repeated correction.",
        "The source material describes a simple model ladder:\n- **Haiku**: lightweight, fast work and straightforward exchanges.\n- **Sonnet**: a general-purpose daily model for a mixture of writing, analysis, and problem-solving.\n- **Opus**: more demanding work that benefits from deeper reasoning and handling of multiple layers.\n- **Fable**: mentioned in the source as an additional high-end model, but its availability and naming should be verified rather than assumed.\n\nThese names and relative positions are not a permanent specification. Anthropic may introduce, rename, retire, or reposition models. Use the model picker and current model documentation to confirm what is available now.",
        "**3.1 Match the model to the job**\nThink of model selection like assigning work to a team. You would not assign every routine administrative task to your most specialised expert. Equally, you would not give a complex strategic decision to a tool that cannot handle the reasoning required.\n\n| Task type | Sensible starting point |\n| --- | --- |\n| Simple rewriting or formatting | Lightweight model |\n| Short summaries and straightforward Q&A | Lightweight model |\n| Routine email drafts | Lightweight or general-purpose model |\n| Brainstorming and first drafts | General-purpose model |\n| Research synthesis and structured analysis | General-purpose model, with stronger reasoning if needed |\n| Complex planning with many constraints | Stronger reasoning model |\n| Multi-step data analysis or difficult technical work | Stronger model, with verification |\n| High-stakes decisions | Model assistance plus qualified human review |\n\nThe best choice depends on the task, not on the prestige of the model name.",
        "**3.2 A simple escalation workflow**\nStart with the least expensive or most efficient model that can reasonably complete the job:\n1. Give it a clear brief and the necessary context.\n2. Review the result against your requirements.\n3. If it misses important constraints, clarify the task once.\n4. If the task genuinely requires deeper reasoning, move to a more capable model.\n5. Keep the final output only after checking it.\n\nDo not repeatedly rerun the same vague prompt on increasingly capable models. Improve the brief first.",
        "**3.3 Make prompts more specific for demanding tasks**\nThe source material stresses that stronger models and research-heavy modes can consume usage more quickly. A precise prompt helps reduce unnecessary work.\n\nA good brief specifies the objective, the context and source material, the exact questions to answer, the expected output format, the scope and exclusions, the level of detail, the standard for evidence, and what to do when information is missing.",
        "**Copy-and-use prompt: bounded analysis brief**\n\n```\nTask:\n[Describe the exact outcome you need.]\n\nContext:\n[Provide the background and relevant source material.]\n\nQuestions to answer:\n1. [Question one]\n2. [Question two]\n3. [Question three]\n\nScope:\n- Include: [topics or data to cover]\n- Exclude: [topics that are not needed]\n- Time period or boundaries: [specify]\n\nOutput:\n[Specify whether you want a table, report, recommendation, plan, or draft.]\n\nQuality requirements:\n- Separate facts from assumptions.\n- Do not invent missing information.\n- Explain uncertainty.\n- Use evidence from the supplied material where possible.\n- Avoid unrelated background.\n- End with the practical implications or next steps.\n```",
        "**3.4 Use research mode deliberately**\nThe source describes a research mode that can search across web resources and assemble a structured report. This is a task mode or capability, not simply another model.\n\nResearch is useful when the job genuinely requires multiple external sources, such as comparing products or services, understanding a market, investigating a topic with several competing explanations, gathering evidence for a strategic decision, or building a source-backed overview. It is unnecessary for many ordinary tasks, such as rewriting a paragraph or organising notes already provided.\n\nBefore starting research, define the scope. Ask for relevant sources, evidence, dates, limitations, and a clear report structure. For important claims, inspect the underlying sources rather than trusting a generated summary.\n\n**Action:** For one week, notice which tasks are simple, routine, or complex. Choose models accordingly and compare the quality and usage experience."
      ]
