    {
      id: "chapter-13",
      number: 13,
      title: "Part V — Universal Task Brief & Operational Checklists",
      paragraphs: [
        "13. The Universal Task Brief\nUse this structure whenever you want Claude to do meaningful work:\n\n```\nOBJECTIVE\nWhat exact outcome do I need?\n\nCONTEXT\nWhat background, files, decisions, and constraints matter?\n\nINPUTS\nWhich sources should you use? Which sources are authoritative?\n\nPROCESS\nWhat steps should you follow?\n\nOUTPUT\nWhat should the final deliverable look like?\n\nQUALITY STANDARD\nWhat makes the result correct, useful, and complete?\n\nBOUNDARIES\nWhat must you not do? What information must you not access?\n\nAPPROVAL\nWhich actions require my review before execution?\n\nUNCERTAINTY\nIf information is missing, what should you ask rather than assume?\n```",
        "14. The Human Approval Checklist\nBefore allowing Claude to take an action, verify:\n- [ ] Is the task clearly defined?\n- [ ] Is Claude using the correct account, folder, Project, or website?\n- [ ] Does it have only the access required?\n- [ ] Have I removed unrelated sensitive material from the workspace?\n- [ ] Have I reviewed the proposed plan?\n- [ ] Could this action send, publish, purchase, delete, overwrite, or commit something?\n- [ ] Have I explicitly approved any consequential action?\n- [ ] Can I undo the action or restore the original?\n- [ ] How will I verify the result?",
        "15. The Skill Quality Checklist\nBefore relying on a reusable Skill, check:\n- [ ] The Skill has a clear purpose.\n- [ ] Its trigger conditions are defined.\n- [ ] Required inputs are documented.\n- [ ] Steps are ordered and specific.\n- [ ] Output format is explicit.\n- [ ] Missing information is handled safely.\n- [ ] It distinguishes facts from assumptions.\n- [ ] It includes quality checks.\n- [ ] It defines approval boundaries.\n- [ ] It has been tested with normal and edge-case inputs."
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "chapter-14",
      number: 14,
      title: "Common Mistakes, Practical Measurement & Long-Term Mastery",
      paragraphs: [
        "16. Common Mistakes to Avoid\n\n**Mistake 1: Treating Claude like a generic chatbot**\nProblem: You repeatedly ask one-off questions and manually reconstruct context.\nBetter approach: Build a useful profile, keep project briefs, and create Skills for recurring processes.\n\n**Mistake 2: Using the strongest model for everything**\nProblem: You consume more usage than necessary on routine tasks.\nBetter approach: Start with an efficient model, improve the prompt, and escalate only when the task warrants it.\n\n**Mistake 3: Giving vague instructions**\nProblem: Claude spends effort guessing what you meant, producing unnecessary output or missing requirements.\nBetter approach: Define the objective, context, scope, output, and quality standard before starting.\n\n**Mistake 4: Confusing memory with a Skill**\nProblem: You expect general personal context to enforce a precise repeatable process.\nBetter approach: Use context for who you are and Skills for how a specific task should be performed.\n\n**Mistake 5: Assuming Projects share all context**\nProblem: You expect a Project to know decisions and preferences that were never made available to it.\nBetter approach: Maintain a portable project brief and verify what the current workspace can access.\n\n**Mistake 6: Connecting every app**\nProblem: You increase the amount of data and permissions involved without a clear benefit.\nBetter approach: Connect only the tools needed for a real workflow and review permissions.\n\n**Mistake 7: Giving browser or computer access too broadly**\nProblem: Claude may encounter unrelated personal or confidential information.\nBetter approach: Limit the scope, close unrelated tabs, use approved folders, and require approval for consequential actions.\n\n**Mistake 8: Automating an unreliable process**\nProblem: A flawed workflow repeats its mistakes more quickly.\nBetter approach: Make the manual process reliable, test it, and only then schedule or automate it.\n\n**Mistake 9: Assuming generated software is production-ready**\nProblem: A working prototype may still contain security, privacy, reliability, or usability problems.\nBetter approach: Test thoroughly and obtain appropriate technical review before deployment.\n\n**Mistake 10: Measuring success by how many features you use**\nProblem: You spend time configuring features that do not solve an important problem.\nBetter approach: Measure time saved, quality improved, errors reduced, and work completed.",
        "17. Measure Whether Claude Is Actually Helping\nThe point of this system is not to maximise AI usage. It is to improve the way work gets done.\n\nTrack a few simple measures:\n\n| Measure | What it tells you |\n| --- | --- |\n| Time per recurring task | Whether the workflow is faster |\n| Rework required | Whether output quality is improving |\n| Errors caught before delivery | Whether review is effective |\n| Tasks completed on time | Whether planning is improving |\n| Usage consumed per useful result | Whether model choice is efficient |\n| Human approval points | Whether risk is being controlled |\n| Hours reclaimed | Whether automation is freeing meaningful time |\n\nFor a recurring task, record the normal time it takes before automation. Compare it with the time spent reviewing Claude's output and correcting it. A task that takes 60 minutes manually but 10 minutes with Claude and 5 minutes of review may be a worthwhile improvement. A task that takes 10 minutes manually but 20 minutes to prompt and correct is not. Do not assume every task should be automated. Some work depends on judgment, relationships, accountability, creativity, or sensitive context. Claude should support those activities, not replace responsibility for them.",
        "Conclusion: Move from Chatting to Delegating\nThe ten capabilities form a progression:\n1. Protect access to the account and the work built around it.\n2. Give Claude useful, reviewed context about you and your work.\n3. Match the model to the complexity of the task.\n4. Turn stable, repetitive procedures into Skills.\n5. Use Projects deliberately and understand their context boundaries.\n6. Connect only the apps that support a real workflow.\n7. Delegate bounded browser tasks with clear approval rules.\n8. Use computer-level workflows for approved files and recurring work.\n9. Build small, useful software with Claude Code.\n10. Create functional layouts and prototypes with Claude Design.\n\nThe biggest change is a change in how you assign work. Instead of asking Claude to answer every question from scratch, define a result, provide the necessary context, give it a repeatable process, and specify where it must stop for your approval.\n\nStart small. Make one workflow reliable. Measure the improvement. Then build the next one. The objective is not to make Claude do everything. It is to spend less time on repetitive work while keeping human judgment, accountability, and control where they matter."
      ]
    }
